  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA closed low can be found over the southern Canadian Prairies \nthis morning, and is expected to dive south across North Dakota \nthrough the day today. On the leading edge of the low, some \nmedium to high chances for light snowfall is anticipated in far \nsouth central North Dakota. Interrogation of BUFKIT model \nsoundings across the James River Valley reveals a mainly dry \ncolumn through much of this morning and early afternoon, though\nslowly becoming more saturated the afternoon and evening. The \noverall window for decent snowfall will be short, however, and \nis expected to limit accumulations to a dusting for the most \npart. Low level stratus that has lingered across the forecast \narea will slowly clear out across the west today through tonight\nas the passage of the low pulls in drier air across the region.\nAlong with ongoing southerly surface winds, this will help \npromote somewhat warmer high temperatures in upper 30s to lower \n40s across the west, while central North Dakota will remain in \nthe lower to mid 30s this afternoon. Clear skies across the west\novernight will allow low temperatures there to drop in the mid \nto upper teens. Low stratus and fog will be possible over \nportions of central into western North Dakota tonight into \nWednesday morning as low, now stalling over eastern South \nDakota, pulls in surface moisture in from the south. With the \nfog and cloud cover, low temperatures across central North \nDakota are expected to remain more in the upper teens to mid \n20s. \n\nWith southwesterly to near zonal flow then expected to develop \non behind this Tuesday system, mainly dry weather and warming \ntemperatures are expected through much of the later half of the \nworkweek. High temperatures on Wednesday begin to warming \nbroadly in the 30s to lower 40s, then jump well in the 40s and \n50s for Thursday and Friday. \n\nFriday into the weekend, a northern stream wave / cutoff low is\nexpected to dive south out of Canada and across the eastern \nnorthern Plains, promoting a general transition to northwesterly\nflow aloft over the forecast area. The passage of this system \nwill loft a surface front across the forecast area, promoting \nlow chances for precpitation late Friday into Saturday. Overall \nQPF potential still appears to low at this time due to limited \nmoisture available within the column. Increasing CAA and \nsteeping low level lapse rates are forecast to promote breezy to\nwindy conditions across the forecast area on Friday, with the \nNBM beginning to advertise near advisory level winds across \nportions of the west. The potential for the strongest winds \nremains a little suspect at this time, as the timing of the CAA \nis a little displaced from the developing moderate to strong low\nlevel jet across North Dakota, but we will need to monitor this\nperiod moving forward. Otherwise, after highs mainly in the 40s\nand 50s Friday, cooling temperatures are anticipated for the \nweekend. The degree of this cooling may depend on where the \npreviously mentioned front stalls and demarcates a baroclinic \nzone, with highs lingering in the 40s and 50s on the warm side \nand then in the 30s and 40s on the cooler side. Cooler air would\nbe expected to prevail in either scenario, with more seasonable\nhighs to start early next week.",
